R CMD INSTALL, R CMD SHLIB and R sessions: the native build tools on Windows

r-zig's Windows build tools are MSYS2's (the build-tools group,
R_HOME/bin/toolchain/usr/bin and the env's Library/usr/bin; etc/Rcmd_environ
and patch 0014 put them first on PATH). r-zig-native-tools ships native
ones instead, built from source with zig: busybox-w32 (sh, sed, awk, grep,
coreutils), GNU make built for Windows32 and pkgconf, in
R_HOME/bin/toolchain/native/bin. Opt-in (user decision U1 b): with
R_ZIG_NATIVE_TOOLS on (1, true or yes, any case; patch 0009's .rzig_on,
the rule of every r-zig on/off variable: any other value but empty, 0,
false and no is off, with a warning), R CMD INSTALL and SHLIB put that
directory first on PATH before they run a configure script or make, and an
R session puts it first as patch 0014 puts the group's, so that
install.packages()'s Sys.which("make") answers the same. CMake's MSYS
Makefiles generator, Rcmd_environ's default, writes /c/ paths for MSYS2's
sh (r-zig's busybox reads them too since its patch 0001, for packages
that name that generator themselves, as RcppParallel), so under the
opt-in an unset or MSYS default generator becomes Unix Makefiles, whose
c:/ paths need no conversion; CONFIG_SHELL defaults to sh, as the
b13-design prototype ran autoconf scripts. etc/Rcmd_environ cannot do this itself: its
${VAR-default} terms test whether a variable is set, not its value, and
the generator has to change with the tools. Without R_ZIG_NATIVE_TOOLS
nothing changes, and on a unix host (R_CROSS_BUILD sets install.R's
WINDOWS) neither. b14-l4 PLAN.md.

